\title{Random-Intercept CoTiMA2 with Study-Level Random Effects:\\
  Phantom Continuous-Time Processes and Their Heterogeneity}
\author{C. Dormann, T. Hagen, \& $\widehat{\textnormal{C}}$. G. Petee}
\date{CoTiMA2 0.14.59 --- 6 November 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

<<setup, include=FALSE, purl=FALSE>>=
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  echo = TRUE, eval = FALSE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE,
  purl = FALSE, comment = "#>", tidy = FALSE, size = "small"
)
options(width = 88)
@

\begin{abstract}
Random-intercept continuous-time meta-analysis (RI CoTiMA) separates stable
between-person differences in construct levels from within-person continuous-
time dynamics. CoTiMA implements one form of RI model by augmenting the state
vector with one time-invariant phantom process per substantive latent process.
The phantom process has zero continuous-time autoregression and zero diffusion.
It contributes either through the measurement model
(\code{randomIntercepts = "MANIFEST"}) or as a time-invariant continuous-time
input (\code{randomIntercepts = "CINT"}). Its freely estimated time-zero
covariance block is the covariance matrix of the person-level random
intercepts. RI CoTiMA-RE additionally treats selected dynamic parameters and
selected elements of this random-intercept covariance structure as varying
between primary studies. This document describes the model, its parameter
mapping, recommended specifications, estimation, diagnostics, and reporting
with \code{ctmaRePrep()} and \code{ctmaReFit()}.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} continuous-time model; individual participant
data meta-analysis; phantom variable; random intercept; study heterogeneity;
ctsem; Stan

\section{Terminology and purpose}

The two occurrences of ``random'' in RI CoTiMA-RE refer to different levels:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{RI} (random intercept) refers to stable differences
    \emph{between persons} in their levels of the substantive variables.
  \item \textbf{RE} (random effects) refers to differences \emph{between
    primary studies} in selected model parameters.
\end{itemize}

These levels must not be collapsed into one set of fixed study indicators.
With \code{backend="julia"}, \code{indVarying="CINT"} (or
\code{"MANIFEST"}) uses augmented time-invariant phantom processes and
estimates one pooled person-level RI covariance matrix.  The baseline phantom
(co-)variances are free; only their TI-predictor effects are fixed to zero.
The phantom variance must not itself be fixed to zero, because that would
remove the person-level random intercept.  Study-level random effects remain a
separate extension: selected study parameters can later be assigned a
between-study distribution and integrated, preferably by a Laplace
approximation, while retaining the same Julia-compatible phantom-state model.
Thus, an RI CoTiMA-RE is a three-level model: repeated observations are nested
within persons, persons are nested within studies, and selected parameters
vary across a population of studies. CoTiMA data are commonly synthesized from
correlation matrices or standardized within measurement occasions. The
manifest means are consequently zero and mean-level heterogeneity between
studies is not the target. The RI component instead concerns the covariance of
stable person-level deviations.

For large RI models, Julia ML uses \code{warm\_start="auto"} by default. The
pilot samples complete persons within studies and estimates only the common
population/base parameters, including the pooled person-level RI covariance;
TI-predictor effects remain zero during this pass. The initial ten-percent
request is enlarged when necessary for parameter count, study coverage,
repeated measurements, or binary response categories. A pilot candidate is
used only if its objective on the complete dataset is finite and no worse than
the ordinary start. User-supplied \code{inits} always take precedence and
disable the pilot. This person-subset warm start is computational
initialization, not the later study-level random-effects distribution or its
Laplace integration.

\section{The ordinary continuous-time process}

Let $s=1,\ldots,K$ index studies and $i=1,\ldots,n_s$ persons. For $m$
substantive latent processes, write
\begin{equation}
  d\bm\eta_{is}(t)
  = \left(\bm A_s\bm\eta_{is}(t)+\bm b_s\right)dt
    + \bm G_s\,d\bm W_{is}(t),
  \label{eq:substantive-sde}
\end{equation}
where $\bm A_s$ is the drift matrix and
$\bm Q_s=\bm G_s\bm G_s^\top$ is the diffusion covariance matrix. A basic
measurement model is
\begin{equation}
  \bm y_{is}(t)=\bm\Lambda\bm\eta_{is}(t)
    +\bm\varepsilon_{is}(t).
  \label{eq:measurement-basic}
\end{equation}
A conventional model may confound persistent between-person level differences
with the temporal relations that are intended to describe within-person
dynamics. The RI construction introduces a vector $\bm r_{is}$ of stable
person-specific components.

\section{Random intercepts as phantom processes}

CoTiMA augments the $m$ substantive processes by $m$ phantom processes:
\begin{equation}
  \bm x_{is}(t)=
  \begin{bmatrix}
    \bm\eta_{is}(t)\\
    \bm r_{is}(t)
  \end{bmatrix},
  \qquad \frac{d\bm r_{is}(t)}{dt}=\bm0.
  \label{eq:augmented-state}
\end{equation}
The phantom processes have zero continuous-time autoregressive effects and no
diffusion innovations. Hence
\begin{equation}
  \bm r_{is}(t)=\bm r_{is}(0)
  \label{eq:constant-ri}
\end{equation}
at every observation occasion. Equivalently, their discrete-time
autoregressive effects are one for every time interval. ``Phantom'' here does
not mean that the component is irrelevant. It means that the additional state
has been introduced to represent a particular source of stable covariance.

The initial covariance of the augmented state can be partitioned as
\begin{equation}
  \bm P_{0,s}=
  \operatorname{Var}
  \begin{pmatrix}\bm\eta_{is}(0)\\\bm r_{is}(0)\end{pmatrix}
  =
  \begin{bmatrix}
    \bm P_{\eta0,s} & \bm C_{\eta r,s}\\
    \bm C_{\eta r,s}^{\top} & \bm\Psi_{r,s}
  \end{bmatrix}.
  \label{eq:t0-blocks}
\end{equation}
The diagonal of $\bm\Psi_{r,s}$ contains the person-level random-intercept
variances. Its off-diagonal elements contain covariances among random
intercepts. The matrix $\bm C_{\eta r,s}$ describes associations between
persons' initial substantive states and their stable random-intercept
components.

\subsection{Manifest-loading construction}

With \code{randomIntercepts = "MANIFEST"}, the augmented drift and measurement
matrices have the conceptual form
\begin{equation}
  \bm A_{\mathrm{MANIFEST},s}=
  \begin{bmatrix}
    \bm A_s & \bm0\\
    \bm0 & \bm0
  \end{bmatrix},
  \qquad
  \bm\Lambda_{\mathrm{MANIFEST}}=
  \begin{bmatrix}\bm\Lambda & \bm\Lambda\end{bmatrix}.
  \label{eq:manifest-construction}
\end{equation}
For a single indicator with unit loading, the observation equation reduces to
\begin{equation}
  y_{is}(t)=\eta_{is}(t)+r_{is}+\varepsilon_{is}(t).
  \label{eq:manifest-observation}
\end{equation}
The stable component therefore enters the observed variable directly at every
occasion.

\subsection{Continuous-intercept construction}

With \code{randomIntercepts = "CINT"}, each phantom process is a constant
input to its corresponding substantive process. In the simplest scaling,
\begin{equation}
  \bm A_{\mathrm{CINT},s}=
  \begin{bmatrix}
    \bm A_s & \bm I_m\\
    \bm0 & \bm0
  \end{bmatrix},
  \qquad
  \bm\Lambda_{\mathrm{CINT}}=
  \begin{bmatrix}\bm\Lambda & \bm0\end{bmatrix}.
  \label{eq:cint-construction}
\end{equation}
The augmented stochastic differential equation contains
\begin{equation}
  d\bm\eta_{is}(t)
  =\left(\bm A_s\bm\eta_{is}(t)+\bm r_{is}\right)dt
   +\bm G_s\,d\bm W_{is}(t).
  \label{eq:cint-sde}
\end{equation}
The phantom process is time invariant, but its effect is expressed through the
continuous-time dynamics rather than through an additional manifest loading.
The two constructions therefore use different parameterizations of stable
person-level differences and should not be assumed to have numerically
identical covariance parameters without the appropriate transformation.

\section{Adding study-level random effects}

Let $p$ identify a selected raw ctsem parameter. RI CoTiMA-RE specifies
\begin{align}
  \xi_{sp} &= \alpha_p + \bm z_s^\top\bm\beta_p + u_{sp},
    \label{eq:study-raw}\\
  \bm u_s &\sim \Normal(\bm0,\bm\Sigma_{\mathrm{study}}),
    \label{eq:study-dist}\\
  \theta_{sp} &= g_p(\xi_{sp}),
    \label{eq:study-transform}
\end{align}
where $g_p$ is the parameter-specific transformation recorded by ctsem.
Study effects are added before transformation so that positivity, stability,
and covariance constraints encoded by ctsem are retained.

For a diagonal phantom-process T0 component, the study-specific random-
intercept variance is schematically
\begin{equation}
  \psi_{r_j,s}=g_{V,j}(\alpha_{V,j}+u_{V,j,s}).
  \label{eq:ri-variance-re}
\end{equation}
For an off-diagonal component, ctsem generally varies an unconstrained
correlation or Cholesky basis and constructs the complete positive-definite
covariance matrix afterwards. It is therefore safer to interpret the final
study-specific covariance matrices than the raw deviations themselves.

\subsection{What \code{person\_varying} means for a phantom process}

The T0 variance of a phantom process is not a person-specific coefficient.
Rather, it is a parameter of the distribution from which person-specific
random intercepts arise:
\begin{equation}
  \bm r_{is}\mid s\sim\Normal(\bm0,\bm\Psi_{r,s}).
  \label{eq:person-ri-distribution}
\end{equation}
Consequently, a study random effect on $\bm\Psi_{r,s}$ represents study
heterogeneity in the amount or covariance pattern of person-level random
intercept variation. \code{ctmaRePrep()} recognizes phantom processes from
T0-mean names ending in \code{\_cint}, such as \code{MeanV1\_cint}. It labels
their selected T0 components as \code{person\_varying = TRUE}, even when the
T0VAR row itself is not marked \code{indvarying} by ctsem.

\section{Selecting random parameters}

If the original CoTiMA model has $m$ substantive processes, its phantom RI
representation contains $2m$ latent processes. The \code{reDRIFT},
\code{reDIFFUSION}, and \code{reT0VAR} masks must therefore be $2m\times2m$.
Only free model parameters can be selected, and covariance elements are
selected in the lower triangle.

\subsection{Univariate example}

Suppose latent 1 is the substantive process and latent 2 is its phantom random
intercept. To let the substantive autoregressive drift and the random-
intercept variance vary between studies, use

<<univariate-masks, purl=FALSE>>=
reDRIFT <- matrix(
  c(TRUE,  FALSE,
    FALSE, FALSE),
  nrow = 2, byrow = TRUE
)

reDIFFUSION <- matrix(FALSE, nrow = 2, ncol = 2)

reT0VAR <- matrix(
  c(FALSE, FALSE,
    FALSE, TRUE),
  nrow = 2, byrow = TRUE
)
@

Here \code{reT0VAR[2,2] = TRUE} allows the person-level RI variance to differ
between studies. The substantive T0 variance \code{reT0VAR[1,1]} remains
without a study random effect. If that variance should also vary, set
\code{reT0VAR[1,1] = TRUE}.

To allow the association between the initial substantive state and the random
intercept to vary, select the lower-triangular covariance:

<<univariate-covariance-mask, purl=FALSE>>=
reT0VAR <- matrix(
  c(FALSE, FALSE,
    TRUE,  TRUE),
  nrow = 2, byrow = TRUE
)
@

This selects \code{T0VAR[2,1]} and \code{T0VAR[2,2]}. The symmetric
upper-triangular cell must remain \code{FALSE}.

\subsection{Multivariate example}

For two substantive processes, assume the ordering
$(\eta_1,\eta_2,r_1,r_2)$. The following mask permits heterogeneity in both
random-intercept variances and their covariance:

<<multivariate-ri-mask, purl=FALSE>>=
reT0VAR <- matrix(FALSE, nrow = 4, ncol = 4)
reT0VAR[3, 3] <- TRUE  # variance of random intercept for V1
reT0VAR[4, 4] <- TRUE  # variance of random intercept for V2
reT0VAR[4, 3] <- TRUE  # covariance between both random intercepts
@

The cross-block cells \code{reT0VAR[3:4,1:2]} concern covariances between the
phantom random intercepts and substantive initial states. They should be
selected only when study heterogeneity in those associations is substantively
intended and identifiable.

\section{Preparation and fitting workflow}

A CoTiMA full fit is required. It may have represented studies using dummy
coding, scaled dummy coding, or weighted effect coding. Extract its pooled
ctsem fit and inspect the augmented latent dimension:

<<extract-fit, purl=FALSE>>=
ctsemFit <- cotimaFit$studyFitList
ctsemFit$argumentList$randomIntercepts
ctsemFit$ctstanmodelbase$n.latent
@

For a univariate substantive model with one phantom process, preparation may
be written as follows:

<<prepare-ri-re, purl=FALSE>>=
prepared <- ctmaRePrep(
  model = ctsemFit,
  study_dummy_ti = paste0("TI", 1:(length(cotimaFit$studyList) - 1)),
  reDRIFT = reDRIFT,
  reDIFFUSION = reDIFFUSION,
  reT0VAR = reT0VAR,
  covariance = "independent",
  parameterization = "noncentered",
  init = "auto"
)

prepared$specification[, c(
  "matrix", "row", "col", "parameter",
  "person_varying", "interpretation", "transform"
)]
@

For \code{Cov\_cint1} at \code{T0VAR[2,2]}, the expected interpretation is
``study-varying variance of a person-level random-intercept pseudo process.''
Recreate \code{prepared} after changing masks or updating the package; an old
prepared object retains its earlier Stan text and mapping.

Bayesian estimation is the primary hierarchical procedure:

<<fit-bayes, purl=FALSE>>=
riReFit <- ctmaReFit(
  prepared,
  estimation = "bayes",
  chains = 4,
  iter = 2000,
  cores = 4,
  fit_args = list(
    warmup = 1000,
    control = list(
      adapt_delta = 0.95,
      max_treedepth = 12
    )
  )
)
@

The iteration counts above are starting values, not universal guarantees.
Short runs with 100 iterations are compilation and propagation checks only;
they are not suitable for substantive inference.

\subsection{Centred and non-centred parameterizations}

The non-centred representation writes $u_{sp}=\tau_p z_{sp}$ with
$z_{sp}\sim\Normal(0,1)$. It is often advantageous when heterogeneity is near
zero or study effects are weakly identified. The centred representation
estimates $u_{sp}\sim\Normal(0,\tau_p^2)$ directly and may be more efficient
when each study strongly identifies its effect. These are computationally
different representations of the same statistical model. RI covariance
components can be weakly identified, so comparing diagnostic runs under both
representations is prudent rather than treating either as universally best.

\subsection{Study-effect covariance structure}

With several selected random parameters, \code{covariance = "independent"}
assumes their study deviations are uncorrelated. \code{"equicorrelated"}
estimates one common correlation, and \code{"full"} estimates every allowed
pairwise correlation. A block structure can separate, for example, dynamic
effects from RI covariance components. Because $K$ is commonly only 10--50,
a full covariance matrix can be poorly identified when many parameters are
random. Independent, equicorrelated, or substantively defined blocks are often
safer starting points.

\section{Summary and interpretation}

<<summary-ri-re, purl=FALSE>>=
riReSummary <- summary(riReFit)

riReSummary$study_sd_raw
riReSummary$study_effects_raw
riReSummary$study_T0VAR
riReSummary$study_T0cov
riReSummary$study_correlations
@

\code{study\_sd\_raw} contains the between-study SDs on ctsem's
unconstrained scale. \code{study\_effects\_raw} contains the study deviations
on that scale. For scalar DRIFT and DIFFUSION transformations,
\code{study\_sd\_transformed} reports the model-implied heterogeneity SD on the
final scale. T0 covariance parameters are deliberately omitted from that
scalar table because their final values depend jointly on the complete
Cholesky/correlation construction.

For random-intercept results, inspect \code{study\_T0VAR} and
\code{study\_T0cov}. In particular, compare the final \code{[2,2]} element in
the univariate augmented model, or the lower-right $m\times m$ block in a
multivariate augmented model. These are the study-specific person-level RI
(co)variances. Raw study effects should not be described as variance
differences on the final scale.

\section{Diagnostics and model checks}

At minimum, report and inspect:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item divergences, maximum-treedepth saturation, and energy diagnostics;
  \item $\widehat R$ and effective sample sizes for population parameters,
    study heterogeneity SDs, and study effects;
  \item whether final study-specific T0 covariance matrices are admissible;
  \item sensitivity to centred versus non-centred parameterization;
  \item sensitivity to the study-effect SD prior and covariance structure;
  \item whether each selected RI covariance component is supported by enough
    studies, persons, and repeated observations; and
  \item posterior predictive checks relevant to stable between-person and
    within-person covariance patterns.
\end{enumerate}

<<hmc-diagnostics, purl=FALSE>>=
rstan::check_hmc_diagnostics(riReFit$engine_fit)

print(
  riReFit$engine_fit,
  pars = c("studyre_sd", "studyre_effect"),
  probs = c(.025, .5, .975)
)
@

No divergences and no maximum-treedepth saturation are desirable. Values of
$\widehat R$ close to one and adequate bulk and tail effective sample sizes are
required. A successful Stan run is not by itself evidence that the random-
intercept heterogeneity is identified.

\section{Recommended reporting}

An RI CoTiMA-RE report should state:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item whether \code{randomIntercepts = "MANIFEST"} or \code{"CINT"} was
    used and how that construction enters the model;
  \item the ordering of substantive and phantom latent processes;
  \item which DRIFT and lower-triangular T0VAR cells received study random
    effects;
  \item which T0 cells represent RI variances, RI covariances, and
    substantive-initial-state--RI covariances;
  \item the study-effect covariance structure, parameterization, and priors;
  \item raw heterogeneity SDs and final study-specific RI covariance matrices;
  \item the number of studies, persons, and measurement occasions; and
  \item convergence, sensitivity, and posterior predictive diagnostics.
\end{enumerate}

The central substantive distinction should remain explicit: RI variance
quantifies stable person-level heterogeneity within a study, whereas its
CoTiMA-RE random effect quantifies how that person-level heterogeneity differs
across studies.

\section{Compact reproducible template}

<<compact-template, purl=FALSE>>=
library(CoTiMA)

# Extract the single pooled ctsem fit from a CoTiMA full fit.
ctsemFit <- cotimaFit$studyFitList

# One substantive latent plus one phantom RI process.
reDRIFT <- matrix(
  c(TRUE,  FALSE,
    FALSE, FALSE),
  2, 2, byrow = TRUE
)

reDIFFUSION <- matrix(FALSE, 2, 2)

# Study-varying person-level random-intercept variance only.
reT0VAR <- matrix(
  c(FALSE, FALSE,
    FALSE, TRUE),
  2, 2, byrow = TRUE
)

prepared <- ctmaRePrep(
  model = ctsemFit,
  study_dummy_ti = paste0(
    "TI",
    seq_len(length(cotimaFit$studyList) - 1L)
  ),
  reDRIFT = reDRIFT,
  reDIFFUSION = reDIFFUSION,
  reT0VAR = reT0VAR,
  covariance = "independent",
  parameterization = "noncentered",
  init = "auto"
)

# Confirm that the pseudo-process variance was recognized correctly.
prepared$specification

riReFit <- ctmaReFit(
  prepared,
  estimation = "bayes",
  chains = 4,
  iter = 2000,
  cores = 4,
  fit_args = list(
    warmup = 1000,
    control = list(
      adapt_delta = 0.95,
      max_treedepth = 12
    )
  )
)

riReSummary <- summary(riReFit)
riReSummary$study_sd_raw
riReSummary$study_T0VAR
riReSummary$study_T0cov
rstan::check_hmc_diagnostics(riReFit$engine_fit)
@

